import json
SETS=[
("interview-presentations","The Center for Teaching and Learning is interviewing students about giving presentations and speaking in front of a class. You have agreed to share your experience and opinions.",[
 ("Tell me about a time you gave a presentation or spoke in front of a group, at school, in a club or at a family event. How did you prepare, and how did it go?","Describe one occasion: what you had to say, how you got ready and one thing that happened while you spoke. An honest account of nerves is as good as a success story."),
 ("Some students prefer to present on their own, while others prefer to present as part of a group where each person speaks for a short time. Which do you prefer, and why?","Pick one and explain what you gain from it. Mentioning one drawback of your choice, and why you still prefer it, strengthens the answer."),
 ("Some people say, \"Being good at presentations is a talent you are born with; practice only helps a little.\" Do you think that is a fair statement? Why or why not?","Respond to the claim about talent versus practice. An example of yourself or someone you know improving, or not, supports your view."),
 ("In the future, do you think presentations at school and work will happen more in person or more as recorded videos? What do you think will cause that change?","Make a clear prediction and give a reason, such as technology, convenience or the value of a live audience."),
]),
("interview-feedback","The Academic Support Center is interviewing students about the feedback they receive on their assignments and tests. You have agreed to answer some questions about your experience and views.",[
 ("Tell me about a time you received comments from a teacher on a piece of work, such as an essay, a test or a project. What did the comments say, and what did you do with them?","Describe one piece of work and one comment you remember, and say honestly whether it changed what you did next."),
 ("Some students prefer written comments they can reread later, while others prefer to talk with the teacher face to face about their work. Which do you find more useful, and why?","State a preference and give a reason based on your own experience. You can say when the other kind of feedback would be better."),
 ("Some people say, \"A grade alone tells students everything they need to know; detailed comments are mostly ignored.\" Do you think that is a fair statement? Why or why not?","Engage with both parts of the claim. You might agree that many comments are ignored but argue about why, with an example."),
 ("Do you think that in the future more feedback on schoolwork will come from computer programs rather than from teachers? Do you think that would be a good change?","Give a prediction and a judgment, with at least one reason for each. Either view is acceptable if it is supported."),
]),
("interview-exploring-the-city","The Student Life Committee is interviewing students about how they explore the town or city around the university in their free time. You have agreed to share your experience and opinions.",[
 ("Tell me about a place outside campus that you visited for the first time recently, such as a shop, park, market or neighborhood. If you have not visited one, describe a place near your home that you would show a visitor. What made it worth the trip?","Choose one place and describe what you did or would do there. Specific details make the answer clear and natural."),
 ("Some students like to plan their free days carefully with a list of places to see, while others prefer to wander and discover places by chance. Which approach do you prefer, and why?","Choose one approach and explain what you gain from it. Mentioning a time your approach worked well, or went wrong, makes the answer stronger."),
 ("Some people say, \"Students who stay on campus most of the time miss half of what their university experience could be.\" Do you think that is a fair statement? Why or why not?","Engage with the claim rather than restating it. You might agree in part and explain what campus offers that the town does not, or the reverse."),
 ("Do you think that in the future people will explore new places more in person or more through photos, videos and online maps? What do you think will drive that change?","Make a prediction and give a reason. You can say what would be gained or lost by the change."),
]),
("interview-online-classes","The university's Office of Digital Learning is interviewing students about taking classes online compared with taking them in person. You have agreed to share your experience and views.",[
 ("Tell me about a class, lesson or training you took online, even a short video course. If you have never taken one, describe how you have learned something from a video or an app. What was that experience like?","Describe one experience in concrete terms: what you did, how you stayed focused, and what you learned. Small examples are fine."),
 ("Some students say they learn more in a classroom with a teacher present, while others say they learn more online, where they can pause and repeat lessons. Which way of learning works better for you, and why?","State a preference and explain it with your own experience. Recognizing one advantage of the other format, and why it matters less to you, strengthens the answer."),
 ("Some people say, \"Online classes are fine for facts, but you can only learn to discuss and argue in a real room with other people.\" Do you think that is a fair statement? Why or why not?","Respond to the distinction the quote makes between facts and discussion. An example of a good or bad online discussion would support either view."),
 ("In ten years, do you think most university classes will be online, in person, or a mix of both? What do you think will decide it?","Give a clear prediction and one or two reasons, such as cost, technology or what students want."),
]),
("interview-student-clubs","The Student Activities Office is interviewing students about clubs and student organizations on campus. You have agreed to answer some questions about your experience and opinions.",[
 ("Tell me about a club, team or group you belong to or once belonged to, at university, school or in your community. If you have not joined one, describe a group you would like to join. What do you do there, or what would you hope to do?","Describe one group and what actually happens there, such as meetings, events or projects. If you have not joined one, explain clearly what attracts you to it."),
 ("Some students prefer to join one club and become deeply involved in it, while others prefer to join several clubs and take part a little in each. Which approach do you think is better, and why?","Pick one approach and explain what it gives you, with a reason drawn from your own experience or what you have seen."),
 ("Some people say, \"Clubs are where students really learn leadership; classes only teach them to follow instructions.\" Do you think that is a fair statement? Why or why not?","Engage with both halves of the claim. You might agree about clubs but question what it says about classes, giving an example."),
 ("Do you think student clubs will become more or less important to university life in the future? What do you think will cause that change?","Make a prediction and explain the reason, such as online communities, busier schedules or new kinds of activities."),
]),
("interview-reading-habits","The university library is interviewing students about reading for pleasure, outside of their required coursework. You have agreed to share your reading habits and opinions.",[
 ("Tell me about something you read recently for enjoyment rather than for a class. It could be a book, a comic, a magazine or a long article. If you do not read for pleasure, describe what you do instead to relax. Why did you choose it?","Describe one recent example and why you chose it. Any kind of reading counts, and so does an honest answer about doing something else."),
 ("Some students prefer printed books, while others prefer reading on a phone, tablet or e-reader. Which do you prefer, and why?","State a preference and give a practical or personal reason. You can mention when you would choose the other format."),
 ("Some people say, \"Students are so busy with assigned reading that expecting them to read for fun is unrealistic.\" Do you think that is a fair statement? Why or why not?","Engage with the reasoning about time and workload. One example from your own week, or from people you know, supports your answer."),
 ("In the future, do you think people will read more or less for pleasure than they do today? What do you think will cause that change?","Give a clear prediction and a reason, such as technology, free time or new formats like audiobooks."),
]),
]
rows=[]
for gid,passage,qs in SETS:
    for q,expl in qs:
        rows.append({"type":"speaking_interview","blanks":None,"prompt":"[Interview] "+q,"choices":[],"graphic":None,"passage":passage,"difficulty":"hard","explanation":expl,"correct_answer":"","passageGroupId":gid,"correct_answers":None,"acceptable_answers":None,"distractor_rationales":[]})
json.dump(rows,open('interview-v3.rows.json','w'),indent=1,ensure_ascii=False)
print(len(rows),"rows",len(SETS),"sets")
